I love battlefield and as a longtime fan  of shooters I finally had a chance to  play hover Junkers on the HTC vive the  vive unlike the oculus rift makes use of  physical rooms and as players running  around a square between sensors in the  case of hover junkers the square could  be the multiplayer lobby which was a  small bar with a robot bartender how do  you a platform at the shooting range or  your actual hover ship used in the  multiplayer combat arena selecting a gun  and holding it with the HTC vive  controller was initially very thrilling  you could flick open the cylinder and  look through it you could turn the gun  over and look at the details from all  angles  they even scribbled words between each  cylinder groove in the revolver better  than that when you fire the gun you  actually need to use sight picture and I  found myself closing one eye to line up  shots as I do on the range I would say  that one adjustment the game probably  needs though is the ability to seat the  gun higher or lower in the palm of your  hand the virtual hands in the game are  tilted a little bit too far forward than  my natural hand which meant I had to  [ __ ] my hand back further than I  normally would to get good sight  position after practicing at the range  mostly shooting and doing manual reloads  I hopped into a round of multi player I  must say the stress of reloading trying  physically to get to your ship's  controls and selecting weapons to shoot  back with fantastic similar to the  active reload mechanic in Gears of War  you have to split your attention to many  different actions while under stress  this simulates the feel of shooting a  real firearm better than any other 2d  counterpart except maybe a receiver a  game specifically designed to simulate  how a firearm or some mechanical now for  the downside of physical shooters in a  real room the cover on the hover  platform is about belly button height so  players have a few pieces of junk  initially to build around them but as  that gets blown away you have to duck  down to get below cover while fantastic  and VR in reality you're constantly  slamming your knees into the hopefully  carpeted ground this game should  probably come with tactical kneepads  also you're constantly spinning around  to engage targets and getting wrapped up  in the HTC vive physical cable this is  not only a tripping hazard but could  break the vive if you stepped on it the  wrong way and let's be honest there are  a lot of middle-aged gamers now you can  actually rent your back playing this  game overall though hover junkers is a  great  of concepts after playing it I firmly  believe the borderland style game would  be possible in VR movement controls  being handled by your left hand worked  really well and going from a hover  platform to just accepting your virtual  feet gliding across the landscape  wouldn't be that big of a jump VR is  incredibly expensive and no particular  fully featured shooter is on the horizon  but hover Drucker's at least in concept  makes me hopeful there is nothing in 2d  gaming right now quite like picking up a  gun in VR closing one eye and squeezing  off a few rounds
 

